"# Setup and Installation\n\nThis guide explains how to set up the environment required to run the experiments in this repository.\n\n## 1. Clone the Repository\n\nClone the repository using Git:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/madaneeyy/cross-domain-vision-model-evaluation.git\n```\n\nNavigate into the project directory:\n\n```bash\ncd cross-domain-vision-model-evaluation\n```\n\n## 2. Create a Virtual Environment\n\nIt is recommended to use a virtual environment to keep the project dependencies isolated.\n\n### Windows\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv\n.venv\\Scripts\\activate\n```\n\n### macOS / Linux\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\n```\n\n## 3. Install Dependencies\n\nInstall the required Python packages using the provided `requirements.txt` file:\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\nSome Vision Mamba dependencies may require additional system or hardware-specific configuration depending on the operating system, Python version, and available CUDA environment.\n\n## 4. Datasets\n\nThis project uses the following datasets:\n\n- CIFAR-100\n- EuroSAT\n- Brain MRI\n\nThe datasets are not included in this repository.\n\nDataset-specific preparation and download instructions will be documented in the `datasets/` directory.\n\n## 5. Running the Experiments\n\nThe experiments are provided as Jupyter notebooks in the `notebooks/` directory.\n\nAfter installing the dependencies, launch Jupyter:\n\n```bash\njupyter notebook\n```\n\nor:\n\n```bash\njupyter lab\n```\n\nOpen the relevant experiment notebook and execute the cells in order.\n\n## 6. Hardware\n\nThe experiments were originally conducted using GPU-based Kaggle environments.\n\nTraining time and resource requirements may vary depending on:\n\n- GPU availability\n- GPU memory\n- CUDA configuration\n- Dataset\n- Model architecture\n- Number of random seeds\n\nA GPU is recommended for reproducing the training experiments, particularly for Vision Transformer and Vision Mamba experiments.\n\n## 7. Reproducibility\n\nThe experimental pipeline uses multiple random seeds to reduce the effect of random initialization and training variation.\n\nThe final experiments were conducted using five random seeds and consistent evaluation procedures across datasets and model architectures.\n\nFurther details about the experimental methodology are available in the project report.\n\n## 8. Project Documentation\n\nAdditional project documentation is available in the `docs/` directory:\n\n- `project_report.pdf` — Final-year project report\n- `project_presentation.pdf` — Final-year project presentation\n- `setup.md` — Environment and setup instructions\n"